Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. *The information contained in
this course is not legal advice and should not be taken as such. The
information presented in this lesson is for educational and information
purposes only. If you need legal advice, please seek council* 

Dispute 21 - Day 1:

Knowing the Difference between the Debt Validation Letter VS Debt
Verification Letter

Bruce, if you are reading this and are unclear of the difference
between the two types of letters and their uses or don't yet feel
comfortable explaining the difference between the two, don't feel
alone. Many Credit Repair Business Owners often get confused with the
terms debt verification and debt validation. They think that this is
just a simple confusion that will not affect the status of the
client's file if they dispute either or, but not understanding the
difference between the two could end up costing your client big time. In
a court of law, it could be the difference between winning and losing a
case. 

Whether I'm answering questions in online groups, mentoring CR
business owners, or training CR staff on credit basics and the dispute
process, the most frequently asked question and most commonly confused
credit terminology I run across is the difference between Verification
and Validation. So, today's lesson will address this topic to help you
gain an understanding of the two terms, their relation to the credit
repair process and the importance or the lack thereof in a court of law.

Because the laws, such as the Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) and Fair
Debt Collection Practices Act (FDCPA), lack clarity in their definition
of many things we often have questions for, such as what constitutes
sufficient evidence or proof of verification and validation, it is wise
to approach each situation based on historical case law (which we will
look at in tomorrow's lesson) and what a court of law would require,
accept, or dismiss. Therefore, one of the most important points to note
upfront is that you want to always begin your approach of the credit
repair and dispute process as if there could possibly be a violation
that may cause the account to end up in a court of law. Doing so will
ensure that the process you follow when working on your client files are
setting them up for success under any circumstance. 

Why People Get Confused with Debt Verification and Debt Validation

One reason why people interchange the two terms is because they are
being used interchangeably. In a nutshell, the two terms refer to a
process wherein a consumer (or the credit repair company acting on
behalf of the consumer) makes a request to a debt collection company to
prove to him/her that they actually owe money. 

Even though the two terms can be used in lieu of one another, there is
an important difference between a legitimate debt validation letter and
a debt verification letter.

Debt Verification

According to the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act, each consumer is
entitled to validate his or her collection debts. However, it does not
include a clear definition as to what constitutes a legitimate debt
validation. 

In lieu of this ruling, a consumer has the ability to send a letter to
his creditor or collectors, 

**requesting the confirmation of information** reflecting his penalized
account. This letter is referred to as a D

**ebt Verification letter**, wherein details included for verification
usually include a debtor's name and address.

Unfortunately, for the creditor or collector, a Debt Verification letter
may not be sufficient evidence to bring to court. This is because anyone
who has access to the internet or a phone book can easily search and
locate an individual's name and address.

As a result, this letter does not have the ability to verify the
consumer's existing debt to his creditor. Moreover, the letter is not
capable of helping put a stop to the harassment from a collection agency
(CA). Having a Debt Verification letter alone is not sufficient to stop
debt collection. The CA can still abide by FDCPA laws and continue
collection activity and taking advantage of individuals who attempt to
use the credit reporting system without understanding it thoroughly.

Debt Validation

Going back to the issue with the FDCPA and its lack of a clear
definition of what compromises a Debt Validation letter, 

**collection agencies can theoretically provide any account
documentation to a consumer**. Since this is considered as adhering to
the law, there are several CA's that use this on consumers who do not
fully understand the difference between such letters. 

But what the court truly requires is the original contract signed by a
consumer addressing the collection agency to provide them a legitimate
debt validation. Because it is not clearly stated in the FDCPA, there
are several CAs that only provide a debt verification letter as a
response to your validation request.

The main difference between the two, however, is the fact that a Debt
Validation letter can demand relevant facts and details that have
commonly exposed fraudulent plans with most collectors.  Even though
the two letters are a part of a debt dispute, consumers can make use of
the debt validation letter to contest any abusive actions of a
collection agency. The debt validation letter can attest to a legitimate
claim on a consumer's account.

What these Letters Can Do In Court

In the event of a court procedure, sufficient proof of validation is the
only acceptable documentation which proves that a consumer owes the
debt. The collection agency will be required to present evidence of this
to the judge.

Because a debt verification letter (name and address) does not prove
anything, it is useless and will not uphold in a court of law. 

So what does this mean for accounts reporting on the credit report?

Without the presence of legitimate debt validation from the
creditor/collector, a consumer has a chance to win his/her case in a
court of law. Moreover, the consumer should no longer receive harassing
phone calls from the collection agency. The same goes for an account on
the credit report. Validation should include specific documentation from
the original creditor. FTC Staff Opinion has concluded that a printout
of the amount owed is not sufficient evidence. If this is all a
collector has provided, they have not conducted a reasonable
investigation to ensure the debt they are attempting to collect on is
actually yours or your client's. When presented with a situation where
a collection company refuses to provide the validation you have
requested, by either stating they are not required to do so or when
trying to pass off verification as being sufficient validation, you can
absolutely demand that the collection company permanently ceases
collection of the account and discontinue its reporting of the account
to any/all credit reporting agencies. 

Reference:

See:**FTC STAFF OPINION Validation of Medical; Debt Verification
required by Section 809(b) - itemized printout; Party responsible for
mailing the verification**

Note:

****(FTC Staff Opinion and other resources that are provided throughout
this course can be used as inserts into your correspondence with the
credit reporting agencies, creditors and collectors. This Opinion letter
or reference of it can be used in your request for validation,
specifically after receiving insufficient evidence or verification
instead of validation from the collection agency. 

